meta {
  name: Create Quote Recap (Foreign Lead)
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/quote-recaps
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "leadId": "000000000000000000000000",
    "policies": [
      { "policyType": "Auto", "premium": 1200.00, "itemCount": 1 }
    ],
    "sameAsHousehold": true,
    "quoteDocument": {
      "key": "{{quoteDocumentKey}}",
      "filename": "carrier-quote.pdf",
      "contentType": "application/pdf",
      "size": 2048
    }
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 404 Not Found — not 403", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(404);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /quote-recaps — lead outside the caller's scope (PAC-39)

  Asserts that a producer cannot attach a recap to a lead they don't own by
  editing the `leadId` in the request. A producer runs on `DataScope.own`, so
  anything not assigned to them is unreachable.

  - **Module:** `quote_recaps`
  - **Permission:** `quote_recaps:write`
  - **Expected:** `404 Not Found`

  ## Why 404 and not 403
  A 403 would confirm that the record exists. Whether another producer's lead
  exists is not the caller's business, so an out-of-scope lead is indistinguishable
  from a missing one — the same shape `ShareLinksService.loadOwnedLink` uses.

  The all-zero id here stands in for "a lead you can't reach"; a real
  out-of-scope lead (say, an owner-assigned one) returns exactly the same status
  and message. **An unassigned lead — one with no `producerId` at all — also
  404s** under `own` scope, which is deliberate: a recap needs an owner.

  The clamp runs **before** any storage verification or household resolution, so
  a probe can't be used to learn anything about the lead either.

  ## Response `404`
  ```json
  { "message": "Lead not found.", "error": "Not Found", "statusCode": 404 }
  ```
}
